================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL
================================================================================

The MIT grant above covers the original work in this repository: the notebooks,
the build tooling, the stylesheet and scripts, the book's prose, and the figures
drawn for it.

It does not cover the files listed below, which are redistributed here under
their own terms. Those terms travel with the files and are not altered by the
licence above. `book/images/CREDITS.md` is the long-form record, including
retrieval dates and the reasoning behind each inclusion.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. book/images/alammar/    — NOT MIT. Non-commercial only.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fourteen diagrams by Jay Alammar, from "The Illustrated Transformer":

    https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/

Licensed by their author under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-
ShareAlike 4.0 International:

    http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/

Cited as the author asks: Alammar, J (2018). The Illustrated Transformer
[Blog post]. Retrieved from https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/

Three consequences, stated plainly because the MIT header above is easy to
read as covering everything:

  * These files may not be used commercially. That restriction binds anyone
    who redistributes this repository, and it binds this repository's own
    publication. If the book ever acquires a commercial dimension —
    sponsorship, a paid tier, a course sold on the back of it — these
    fourteen files must be removed first.

  * They are reproduced unmodified, and must stay that way. Under CC 4.0
    section 2(a), including unmodified material in a larger collection does
    not create Adapted Material, which is what keeps the ShareAlike term from
    reaching the rest of this repository. Cropping, recolouring, relabelling
    or redrawing any of them changes that answer.

  * Attribution appears on each figure in the rendered book, and the build
    refuses to publish a figure that has lost its credit line.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. book/images/transformer-explainer/    — MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Five figures from Transformer Explainer by the Polo Club of Data Science at
Georgia Tech:

    https://poloclub.github.io/transformer-explainer/
    https://github.com/poloclub/transformer-explainer

    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2022 Polo Club of Data Science.

The full notice is kept verbatim alongside the files, in
book/images/transformer-explainer/LICENSE.txt, which is what the licence
requires of anyone redistributing them. Do not delete it.

Paper: Cho, A., Kim, G. C., Karpekov, A., Helbling, A., Wang, Z. J., Lee, S.,
Hoover, B., and Chau, D. H. "Transformer Explainer: Learning LLM Transformers
with Interactive Visual Explanation and Experimentation." CHI 2026.
arXiv:2408.04619.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. book/vendor/katex/    — MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

KaTeX, which renders the book's mathematics offline so the page needs no
network access:

    https://katex.org  /  https://github.com/KaTeX/KaTeX

    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2013-2020 Khan Academy and other contributors.

The full notice is kept at book/vendor/katex/LICENSE.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Linked, not included
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Christopher Olah's "Understanding LSTMs" carries no licence statement — none on
the post, none on his About page, and no LICENSE file in his site's repository —
so none of its diagrams are reproduced here and none should be added without
written permission. Chapter 05 links to the post, credits the "conveyor belt"
reading of the cell state to him, and uses a diagram drawn for this book.

    https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-08-Understanding-LSTMs/

The NLTK corpora the notebooks use (movie_reviews, gutenberg, comtrans, punkt)
are downloaded at runtime to ~/nltk_data/ and are not redistributed here. Each
carries its own licence, which `nltk.download()` reports.
